{"maxId":158,"entries":{"chapter-22-study-medication-prescribing-and-de-prescribing|Chapter 22 Study: Medication Prescribing and De-Prescribing":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"copy|Older adults have greater drug exposure and vulnerability to adverse drug events; female sex, frailty, cognitive impairment and increased drug utilization add risk (p301–302).":["u3"],"copy|Include an adverse drug effect in the differential for a new symptom. Reconcile medications, allergies, doses, interactions and renal adjustment (p301–302).":["u4"],"copy|Pharmacokinetics describes drug handling; renal clearance and hepatic metabolism can change with aging. Pay particular attention to lipid-soluble, CYP-metabolized and renally excreted drugs (p302–303).":["u5"],"copy|Pharmacodynamic sensitivity may increase independently: benzodiazepines, opioids and warfarin are examples. Age and sex both matter (p303).":["u6"],"copy|Use weight- and renal-based dosing where appropriate, particularly with narrow therapeutic indices; estimated filtration and prescribing decision support can help (p303).":["u7"],"copy|Beers and STOPP/START support decisions alongside patient preferences, pharmacy review and other clinical tools (p303).":["u8"],"copy|DRUGS: Discuss goals, Review medications, Use tools/frameworks, apply a Geriatric approach, and Stop medications where appropriate (p313).":["u9"],"copy|A prescribing cascade starts when a drug adverse effect is mistaken for a new illness and another drug is added. Recognize and manage the original cause (p304).":["u10"],"copy|Balance overuse, underuse and misuse; goals, organ function and expected benefit can change over time (p306, p315).":["u11"],"copy|Aim for the simplest effective regimen and minimum effective doses. A medication grid can help reduce complexity (p313–315).":["u12"],"copy|For dementia-related behavioral symptoms, nonpharmacologic approaches are usually recommended first: caregiver education, problem solving and interventions directed at specific triggers (p312).":["u13"],"copy|Review cost and actual adherence, not only the prescription list; a prescribed medication may never be dispensed or taken regularly (p311).":["u14"],"copy|NSAIDs can cause gastrointestinal bleeding and renal impairment; for osteoarthritis consider exercise/weight reduction, and acetaminophen before NSAIDs when medication is needed (p312).":["u15"],"copy|Tolerating a drug does not prove ongoing benefit. Long-term digoxin with normal systolic function illustrates potential risk without benefit (p312).":["u16"],"copy|Trial participants may poorly represent frail, multimorbid older adults and older women. Inappropriate prescribing includes omission of beneficial therapy, not only excessive medication (p309, p315).":["u17"],"exam-cited-book-images|Exam-cited book images":["u18"],"table-22-2-book-image|Table 22-2 — book image":["u19"],"copy|Hazzard 8e, Table 22-2, p.305":["u20","u35"],"exam-questions|Exam questions":["u21"],"copy|2023-Jun Q79":["u22"],"copy|2023-Jun Q98":["u23","u33"],"copy|2026-Jun Q80":["u24"],"copy|CHAPTER 22s / HAZZARD":["u25"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 22 - Medication Prescribing and De-Prescribing; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u26"],"copy|Prescribing risk":["u27"],"copy|Assessment & drug handling":["u28"],"copy|Management & deprescribing":["u29"],"copy|Drug cautions & exam traps":["u30"],"copy|Book images cited by past exams.":["u31"],"copy|Tap an image to zoom.":["u32"],"copy|Past-exam questions linked to this chapter (3):":["u34"],"copy|Lipid-soluble benzodiazepines have a larger volume of distribution in older adults, who may also be more sensitive to their effects (Ch 22, pp302–303).":["u81"],"copy|June 2026 Q10 selected clonazepam for review.":["u84"],"copy|Check the original indication and duration: chronic sedative-hypnotic use may cause physical dependence and withdrawal on discontinuation (Ch 11, p169).":["u85"],"copy|Ch 23, p319, gives 13% as the estimated prevalence of benzodiazepine use in older adults.":["u154"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): benzodiazepines increase cognitive and fall risks in older adults; assess the indication and applicable exceptions — AGS Beers Criteria 2023, Table 2.":["u155"],"copy|For nursing-home residents with dementia, reserve antipsychotics for situations in which the benefit clearly outweighs the risk, such as behavior posing a risk to the resident or others.":["u156"],"copy|Reassess the need and consider dose reduction or discontinuation (p308).":["u157"],"copy|Review the reason for prolonged acid suppression, including exceptions such as erosive esophagitis (pp303–304, 312).":["u109"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): the 2019 Beers criteria make an explicit exception for bipolar disorder in their antipsychotic warning.":["u105"],"copy|The 2023 AGS Beers Criteria reserve antipsychotics for dementia-related behavior when nonpharmacologic measures fail or cannot be used and the person threatens substantial harm to self or others — AGS Beers Criteria 2019, Table 2 and AGS Beers Criteria 2023, Table 2.":["u158"],"copy|CHAPTER 22 / HAZZARD":["u25"]},"sequenceOffsets":[[159,-123],[204,-117],[222,-112],[266,0]]}
